# Pronouns Reference Content

Cases in action: the words you'll use most frequently in Greek.

## Purpose

Show pronouns as the practical application of cases. The forms are a lookup; the skills are picking the right word for the job and putting it in the right place.

## Key Insight

English uses one "me" for three jobs. Greek gives each job its own word (με, μου, εμένα), and each word a fixed place in the sentence. μου is the surprise: it covers "to me" as well as "my".

---

## Hero

<hero component="ReferenceHero" file="tabs/pronouns.tsx">
<title>Every “me” has its own word.</title>
<thesis>English uses one “me” for three jobs. Greek gives each job its own word, and each word a fixed place in the sentence. Get the word and the place right, and the rest is a lookup.</thesis>
</hero>

## Three Words for "Me" (first band)

<teaching-cards source="PRONOUN_JOBS in @/constants/pronouns">
Same idiom as the Cases tab's three roles: one TeachingCard per word, tinted in its case scheme, the Greek form as the title, the learner handle as the badge. Examples are MarkedGreek, so only the pronoun carries the case colour. The cards are the page's colour key.

| Title | Badge          | Scheme          | Job                                                              | Examples |
|-------|----------------|-----------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|----------|
| με    | Target · short | case-accusative | The action lands on me. It sits right before the verb.           | με βλέπει (he sees me) |
| μου   | Owner          | case-genitive   | “My”, and also “to me”. After a noun it owns; before a verb it receives. | το σπίτι μου (my house), μου λέει (he tells me) |
| εμένα | Target · long  | case-accusative | After a preposition, or when “me” carries the stress.            | για εμένα (for me) |

</teaching-cards>

<notes source="PRONOUN_NOTES in components/pronouns-section.tsx">
Ruled list under the cards, pronouns in their case colour:
- Not sure which? A preposition in front means εμένα; if English could say "to", μου; otherwise με.
- μας and σας do double duty: one word for Target and Owner (μας βλέπει, το σπίτι μας).
- The Doer usually drops out: θέλω is "I want"; εγώ and εσύ come back for contrast (εγώ θέλω τσάι, εσύ;).
- Formal "you" is plural: σας ευχαριστώ, η γνώμη σας.
</notes>

