## Placement (band: "Short pronouns hug the verb.")

<section name="clitic-placement" anchor="#clitic-placement">
The practice placement drill links here (`practice/pronouns/placement.tsx`), so the band keeps `id="clitic-placement"`.

An aligned slot table in a bordered Card (STATEMENTS and COMMANDS, local to the section). Columns: lead-in (muted, right-aligned) · pronoun · verb · pronoun after the verb · English. The pronoun column never moves, whatever stands in front of it; under "Commands put it after" the pronoun lands in the fourth column instead. Pronouns carry their case colour.

| Lead-in | Pronoun | Verb   | After  | English |
|---------|---------|--------|--------|---------|
|         | με      | βλέπει |        | he sees me |
| θα      | με      | δει    |        | he will see me |
| δεν     | με      | βλέπει |        | he doesn't see me |
| θέλω να | με      | δεις   |        | I want you to see me |
|         | μου το  | δίνει  |        | he gives it to me |
| *Commands put it after* |||||
|         |         | δες    | με     | look at me |
|         |         | δώσε   | μου το | give it to me |

Below `sm` the English column is hidden and each row's English drops to a line beneath it.

Notes: with two, the person comes first (μου το); "my" goes after the noun and the article stays (το σπίτι μου, η μητέρα σου).
</section>

---

## Look it up

<paradigm name="pronoun-forms" component="PronounTable">
One GrammarTable, Singular and Plural as row groups under one header. Rows are named by the English Doer (I, you, he, she, it / we, you (pl.), they ×3 with the gender beneath). Columns, each a chip in its case colour with a line beneath:

| Column         | Source             | Beneath              | Weight |
|----------------|--------------------|----------------------|--------|
| Doer           | SUBJECT_PRONOUNS   | usually dropped      | muted  |
| Target · short | OBJECT_PRONOUNS    | before the verb      | semibold |
| Owner          | POSSESSIVE_PRONOUNS| my, to me            | semibold |
| Target · long  | EMPHATIC_PRONOUNS  | after a preposition  | normal |

Below `sm` the chips become case-coloured text and the line beneath shows only short / long. The four paradigms are read row by row, so `pronouns.test.ts` checks they list the same persons in the same order.

Footnote: after a preposition εμένα and εσένα often lose their first ε (για μένα, χωρίς σένα).
</paradigm>

Then the shared "Article or pronoun?" callout (`HomographCallout`, id `article-or-pronoun`), also on the Articles tab.

<section name="indefinites" heading="Someone, nothing, everyone">
A 3×3 GrammarTable: rows thing / person / place, columns some / no, any / every. Laid out as a grid, the κάπ- of "some" and the όλ- of "every" show without being taught.

|        | some    | no, any | every  |
|--------|---------|---------|--------|
| thing  | κάτι    | τίποτα  | όλα    |
| person | κάποιος | κανένας | όλοι   |
| place  | κάπου   | πουθενά | παντού |

</section>

<phrases heading="Phrases to learn whole" source="PRONOUN_PHRASES, grouped by category">
Ruled lists per category (Requests, Opinions, Questions, Answers, Family), neutral Greek: πες μου, δώσε μου, περίμενέ με, βοήθησέ με, άκουσέ με · μ' αρέσει, δε μ' αρέσει, μου φαίνεται · τι σου φαίνεται;, πώς σε λένε; · με λένε... · σ' αγαπώ, μου λείπεις.
</phrases>

